---
title: Actor variables
sidebar:
  order: 9
---

Each actor has 16 variables, numbered 0 to 15, that each hold an integer. Use them to track an actor's state between EVT scripts, share information with other actors, and configure an actor from its formation.

## Setting and getting variables

Use `SetActorVar` and `GetActorVar`:

```c
Call(SetActorVar, ACTOR_SELF, 0, 1)
Call(GetActorVar, ACTOR_SELF, 0, LVar0)  // LVar0 = 1
```

## Naming variables

Define an enum to give your variables readable names:

```c
enum ActorVars {
    AVAR_Toppled     = 0,
    AVAR_ToppleTurns = 1,
};

// in EVS_Init:
Call(SetActorVar, ACTOR_SELF, AVAR_Toppled, false)

// in EVS_TakeTurn:
Call(GetActorVar, ACTOR_SELF, AVAR_Toppled, LVar0)
IfTrue(LVar0)
    // try to get back up instead of attacking
EndIf
```

## Communicate between actors

An actor can store another actor's ID in a variable to keep track of it. Magikoopa stores the ID of the clone it makes, and later checks whether the clone is still in the battle:

```c
// in the attack that makes a clone
Call(SummonEnemy, Ref(CloneFormation), false)
Call(SetActorVar, ACTOR_SELF, AVAR_CloneActorID, LVar0)

// in EVS_Idle
Call(GetActorVar, ACTOR_SELF, AVAR_CloneActorID, LVar0)
Call(ActorExists, LVar0, LVar1)
IfEq(LVar1, true)
    // the clone is still in the battle
EndIf
```

For the full pattern, see `battle/actor/magikoopa.c`.

To read or set another actor's variable, pass its actor ID in place of `ACTOR_SELF`. Dry Bones does this to check whether the other Dry Bones in the battle have collapsed:

```c
Call(GetActorVar, LVar0, AVAR_Collapsed, LVar3)
```

## Configuring an actor from its formation

The formation sets variables 0 to 3 when the actor spawns, from its `.var0`, `.var1`, `.var2`, and `.var3` fields. Fields you leave out are `0`. This lets one actor behave differently depending on the battle it's in.

To set a variable from a formation, add the field after the priority:

```c
static Formation frost_goombas = {
    OVL_ACTOR_BY_IDX("frost_goomba", BTL_POS_GROUND_B, 10),
    OVL_ACTOR_BY_IDX("frost_goomba", BTL_POS_GROUND_C, 9, .var0 = true),
};
```

Bullet Bill uses this. In a normal battle, its formation leaves `.var0` as `0`. Before Bill Blaster fires one, the blaster sets `.var0` of its Bullet Bill formation to `true` and `.var1` to its own actor ID. Bullet Bill reads the variables in its `EVS_Init` to choose where it starts:

```c
enum ActorVars {
    AVAR_FiredFromBlaster   = 0,
    AVAR_BlasterID          = 1,
};

EvtScript EVS_Init = {
    Call(BindTakeTurn, ACTOR_SELF, Ref(EVS_TakeTurn))
    Call(BindIdle, ACTOR_SELF, Ref(EVS_Idle))
    Call(BindHandleEvent, ACTOR_SELF, Ref(EVS_HandleEvent))
    Call(GetActorVar, ACTOR_SELF, AVAR_FiredFromBlaster, LVar0)
    IfFalse(LVar0)
        // float a little above its home position
    Else
        // start at the mouth of the blaster
        Call(GetActorVar, ACTOR_SELF, AVAR_BlasterID, LVar0)
        Call(GetActorPos, LVar0, LVar1, LVar2, LVar3)
        // ...
    EndIf
    Return
    End
};
```

For the full pattern, see `battle/actor/bullet_bill.c` and `battle/actor/bill_blaster.c`.

For bigger differences, such as a different type, HP, or status table, give the actor a variant instead. Each variant is a blueprint defined with `OVL_DEF_ACTOR(name)`, and a formation selects one by [writing the actor's name and the variant's name](/papermario-dx/battles/formations/#placing-actors), such as `"anti_guy:trio"`.
